<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sirpur, Chhattisgarh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shripura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sirpur is a village on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanadi">Mahanadi</a> river in Mahasamund district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the site of ancient Shripura, capital of the Panduvamshi kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakshina-kosala">Dakshina Kosala</a> from the 6th century. Excavations have uncovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples, Buddhist monasteries, a Jain shrine and a market area, and the brick Lakshmana Temple still stands. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> is thought to have visited the city in the 7th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sirpur-chhattisgarh.jpg" alt="Sirpur, Chhattisgarh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:7th_-_9th_century_Surang_tila_mandapa_before_shrines,_Sirpur_monuments_Chhattisgarh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
